Build configuration values let a project or source module receive typed primitive values before its modules are compiled.

**Current support versus accepted design.** Declaration-owned `#Config of T` inputs and typed `--input` values are supported today with the existing profile-based numeric types. The accepted exact-numeric contract expands inputs to all fixed integer and binary-float types and preserves raw CLI numeric spelling until the exact contract is known; implementation remains pending. `$config` prefixes and explicit-only providers stay queued. See @../progress (Progress).

### Accepted `$config`

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $config
    analytics #Bool = false

    if analytics:
        send_analytics()
    ;
]

The name is the input name, the type is the contract and `=` is the fallback. A required non-optional input may omit the initializer. Ordinary constants still need one.

Only `$config` declarations create explicit-only contracts. A `$project` field named `version` does not supply a source input named `version`.

### Current `#Config`

Declaration-owned `#Config of T` is current; the `$config` prefix and explicit-only providers remain queued:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    analytics #Config of Bool = false
]

### Command examples

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    moth build . --input enabled=true
    moth build . --input retries=4
    moth build . --input api_url=https://example.com
]

Raw CLI numeric spelling keeps its category until the exact input contract is known. A whole literal can materialise directly as `U64` or `F32`; decimal/exponent spelling cannot satisfy an integer contract. Already typed programmatic inputs keep their exact type.

Build configuration cannot inspect operating-system, architecture, backend or target identity.
